00:17:32.640 --> 00:17:36.000
and going for a championship
and winning and all that stuff.

00:17:36.000 --> 00:17:38.600
How did that set you up as a human being?

00:17:38.600 --> 00:17:40.340
- Okay, (clears throat) good question.

00:17:40.340 --> 00:17:43.110
Let me just, first of all,
kind of put in context,

00:17:43.110 --> 00:17:47.320
the environment of NFL when
I joined as opposed to today

00:17:47.320 --> 00:17:49.700
because I think the
biggest part of us being

00:17:49.700 --> 00:17:52.620
excited about our future
is knowing about our past,

00:17:52.620 --> 00:17:55.420
and it was something that, Carl
Mark said that said it all.

00:17:55.420 --> 00:17:57.550
The first Browder ground
is rewriting your history.

00:17:57.550 --> 00:17:59.110
If they steal our past,

00:17:59.110 --> 00:18:01.390
we truly do not have an
appreciation for where we are

00:18:01.390 --> 00:18:03.473
and we have no vision
for where we're going.

00:18:03.473 --> 00:18:04.306
(clears throat)

00:18:04.306 --> 00:18:05.139
So that being in context,

00:18:05.139 --> 00:18:07.713
again, I grew up in the Deep
South, there's a segregation.

00:18:07.713 --> 00:18:09.660
At 12 years old, I remember marching.

00:18:09.660 --> 00:18:11.250
I was the youngest person at that time.

00:18:11.250 --> 00:18:13.140
I was with the Florida
Indian when my dad was

00:18:13.140 --> 00:18:14.400
a college professor.

00:18:14.400 --> 00:18:15.930
In front of Florida state theater

00:18:15.930 --> 00:18:19.418
because we couldn't go in to
look at a movies theater there.

00:18:19.418 --> 00:18:20.251
(clears throat)

00:18:20.251 --> 00:18:22.100
My dad... You know, just
a little extra background.

00:18:22.100 --> 00:18:23.343
My dad actually came back from war

00:18:23.343 --> 00:18:26.560
a very proud world war II vet.

00:18:26.560 --> 00:18:29.620
Could not go to get his
postgraduate down in Texas

00:18:29.620 --> 00:18:31.360
because of the Jim Crow laws,

00:18:31.360 --> 00:18:33.930
and I remember running
across a box of letters

00:18:33.930 --> 00:18:37.150
when he passed away of all
these rejection letters

00:18:37.150 --> 00:18:38.880
that had gotten across the country

00:18:38.880 --> 00:18:41.710
that would not accept
him to those colleges.

00:18:41.710 --> 00:18:44.830
And I looked through it and I
realize it's a racist thing,

00:18:44.830 --> 00:18:46.550
but he never ever brought it out.

00:18:46.550 --> 00:18:48.010
He'd never talked about it.

00:18:48.010 --> 00:18:50.575
For him, it was motivation, (laughs)

00:18:50.575 --> 00:18:51.660
and that's what those folks do.

00:18:51.660 --> 00:18:52.610
There was motivation.

00:18:52.610 --> 00:18:54.040
He ended up going to a house state

00:18:54.040 --> 00:18:55.650
where he's got his PhD.

00:18:55.650 --> 00:18:57.680
He ended up being a 40
year professor there,

00:18:57.680 --> 00:18:58.960
and was an entrepreneur, researched,

00:18:58.960 --> 00:18:59.950
and we traveled around the world.

00:18:59.950 --> 00:19:03.032
I was five years old,
living in Liberia, Africa.

00:19:03.032 --> 00:19:03.959
- Wow!

00:19:03.959 --> 00:19:04.792
- Again, that's the environment.

00:19:04.792 --> 00:19:06.205
This is the 50s.

00:19:06.205 --> 00:19:08.840
I had an older uncle, and by the way,

00:19:08.840 --> 00:19:12.050
he had his older brother, both
got their PhDs at Ohio state.

00:19:12.050 --> 00:19:15.570
Dad got in Agronomy, uncle
Lima, got it in Economics.

00:19:15.570 --> 00:19:17.490
He ended up teaching at
the University of Houston.

00:19:17.490 --> 00:19:18.323
(clears throat)

00:19:18.323 --> 00:19:19.900
Their oldest brother was
probably the smartest one.

00:19:19.900 --> 00:19:23.140
He was an engineer and he loved planes,

00:19:23.140 --> 00:19:25.020
but he couldn't do that
when he went to service.

00:19:25.020 --> 00:19:28.109
So on a part time base he
ended buying a little...

00:19:28.109 --> 00:19:29.560
This was 1962.

00:19:29.560 --> 00:19:32.290
Nobody bought planes in 1962, (laughs)

00:19:32.290 --> 00:19:33.590
particularly, black folks,

00:19:34.537 --> 00:19:36.404
but he bought a little plane

00:19:36.404 --> 00:19:37.237
and because he was entrepreneurial,

00:19:37.237 --> 00:19:40.220
he was an engineer, but he
lived in Wichita Falls, Texas.

00:19:40.220 --> 00:19:42.530
He took letters from Wichita Falls base,

00:19:42.530 --> 00:19:43.860
the military based in Chicago,

00:19:43.860 --> 00:19:46.182
that was the start time business,

00:19:46.182 --> 00:19:48.256
the first FedEx. (laughs)

00:19:48.256 --> 00:19:51.310
And I remember him coming,
flying to Tallahassee,

00:19:51.310 --> 00:19:52.940
and I must have been maybe 12 years old

00:19:52.940 --> 00:19:55.530
when I had my first
flight in an aeroplane.,

00:19:55.530 --> 00:19:57.450
and I remember that experience very well

00:19:57.450 --> 00:19:59.270
because he took me up

00:19:59.270 --> 00:20:01.920
and I don't know if you
know what the stall...

00:20:01.920 --> 00:20:04.250
I'm sure you know what stall
means, what it's all about,

00:20:04.250 --> 00:20:05.700
but if you don't know what a stall means,

00:20:05.700 --> 00:20:07.110
it's good to know before you stall

00:20:07.110 --> 00:20:09.546
or before you get the experience.

00:20:09.546 --> 00:20:11.212
(Dave laughs) It feels like
falling out of the air,

00:20:11.212 --> 00:20:13.260
and I remember stalling, getting panicked,

00:20:13.260 --> 00:20:14.630
and he kind of got control,

00:20:14.630 --> 00:20:17.660
smiled at me and taught
me what airplane lift was.

00:20:17.660 --> 00:20:21.202
I know today exactly what
airplane lift means. (laughs)

00:20:21.202 --> 00:20:24.040
But I'm making that point--
It ain't fun, either way.

00:20:24.040 --> 00:20:25.180
- No. No

00:20:25.180 --> 00:20:28.460
I'm making that point
because I want people to know

00:20:28.460 --> 00:20:31.320
that my community was truly
one of the most competitive

00:20:31.320 --> 00:20:33.102
communities in our country,

00:20:33.102 --> 00:20:34.500
one they were so proud of.

00:20:34.500 --> 00:20:36.150
We educated ourselves because we knew

00:20:36.150 --> 00:20:38.860
that's what we needed to do to compete.

00:20:38.860 --> 00:20:41.830
My degree was in biology,
my minor was chemistry.

00:20:41.830 --> 00:20:43.850
Why was I comfortable do that?

00:20:43.850 --> 00:20:45.320
Because my dad was a cosmic fester.

00:20:45.320 --> 00:20:47.660
He had a laboratory, I
feel very comfortable,

00:20:47.660 --> 00:20:50.670
and the most important thing
is when I was told by someone

00:20:50.670 --> 00:20:54.500
in high school that I could
not do that, I made it my duty.

00:20:54.500 --> 00:20:56.060
I lived in the library
of University of Miami

00:20:56.060 --> 00:20:57.260
to prove that guy wrong

00:20:57.260 --> 00:20:59.629
because that's the
attitude we had those days.

00:20:59.629 --> 00:21:00.712
(clears throat)

00:21:00.712 --> 00:21:01.545
So that was the environment.

00:21:01.545 --> 00:21:03.732
Again, I went through University of Miami.

00:21:03.732 --> 00:21:05.400
I was drafted through
the Jets. (clears throat)

00:21:05.400 --> 00:21:06.573
When I got to the Jets,

00:21:08.120 --> 00:21:10.620
this is the way it was in those days.

00:21:10.620 --> 00:21:13.180
There was no black quarterbacks, centers,

00:21:13.180 --> 00:21:16.500
pre-safeties or middle linebackers.

00:21:16.500 --> 00:21:19.710
In those days the message
of the democratic party

00:21:19.710 --> 00:21:21.290
was that black people couldn't think.

00:21:21.290 --> 00:21:22.390
We were not leaders.

00:21:22.390 --> 00:21:25.210
We could run, we could
jump, we could play music,

00:21:25.210 --> 00:21:28.940
but thinking was out of our
wheelhouse. (clears throat)

00:21:28.940 --> 00:21:30.453
By the way, to that point.

00:21:31.440 --> 00:21:33.420
When you go back and
see Martin Luther King,

00:21:33.420 --> 00:21:34.450
and you see the marching.

00:21:34.450 --> 00:21:36.520
I want you now to keep this
in context when you see them,

00:21:36.520 --> 00:21:39.420
you'll notice he has
white shirts, black ties,

00:21:39.420 --> 00:21:41.600
sometimes business caps
and always business shoes

00:21:41.600 --> 00:21:43.370
because they were going
against the narrative

00:21:43.370 --> 00:21:45.743
of the leftist, of the racist,

00:21:47.998 --> 00:21:48.831
of illiterate,

00:21:49.670 --> 00:21:52.283
lazy, bumpkins, can't talk,

00:21:53.190 --> 00:21:55.260
all of the negatives you
can possibly think of,

00:21:55.260 --> 00:21:57.230
and they were showing
in the way they marched

00:21:57.230 --> 00:21:58.750
and the discipline and non-violent

00:21:58.750 --> 00:22:00.570
that they were totally off base,

00:22:00.570 --> 00:22:03.830
and they wanted to make
sure their kids understood

00:22:03.830 --> 00:22:04.850
not to take the narrative of

00:22:04.850 --> 00:22:07.030
these people, not to be
proud of who you are.

00:22:07.030 --> 00:22:08.820
So I just want to put that in there.

00:22:08.820 --> 00:22:11.960
So when I think back about
when I came to the NFL.

00:22:11.960 --> 00:22:14.330
- What year was your rookie year?

00:22:14.330 --> 00:22:17.060
- 1973. (clears throat) 1973.

00:22:17.060 --> 00:22:21.050
I now look at in NFL now
that's based on meritocracy,

00:22:21.050 --> 00:22:22.320
color doesn't matter.

00:22:22.320 --> 00:22:23.710
That means no matter what color you are,

00:22:23.710 --> 00:22:24.880
how fat, how big you are,

00:22:24.880 --> 00:22:26.790
if you can bring merit to that team,

00:22:26.790 --> 00:22:28.770
you'll get yourself a good contract.

00:22:28.770 --> 00:22:30.760
That speaks volumes that people understand

00:22:30.760 --> 00:22:32.960
how far we've come, then all of a sudden,

00:22:32.960 --> 00:22:35.150
then we're a racist place
doesn't work anymore.

00:22:35.150 --> 00:22:36.560
I mean, I know what racism look like.

00:22:36.560 --> 00:22:38.430
I know what systemic racism looks like.

00:22:38.430 --> 00:22:41.190
We have an opportunity right
now to do anything we want to.

00:22:41.190 --> 00:22:42.940
I can not only go into a theater,

00:22:42.940 --> 00:22:45.630
I can own a series of them if I wanted to,

00:22:45.630 --> 00:22:48.320
but it comes down to
recognizing (clears throat)

00:22:48.320 --> 00:22:49.650
that the differential

00:22:49.650 --> 00:22:51.280
and every single race, every single person

00:22:51.280 --> 00:22:54.080
that translates to success is
based on those four tenets.

00:22:54.080 --> 00:22:55.170
Very simple to understand,

00:22:55.170 --> 00:22:57.220
and I think it's time that we find a way

00:22:57.220 --> 00:23:01.730
to explain conservatism,
explain the American way,

00:23:01.730 --> 00:23:03.970
the entree to the middle class, simply.

00:23:03.970 --> 00:23:05.667
We spend too much time
trying to debate it,

00:23:05.667 --> 00:23:07.380
but it comes to the four tenets;

00:23:07.380 --> 00:23:09.890
Head, heart, hands and home.

00:23:09.890 --> 00:23:13.240
Education, faith, industry and family.

00:23:13.240 --> 00:23:14.690
It's that simple.

00:23:14.690 --> 00:23:16.530
If we educate ourselves,

00:23:16.530 --> 00:23:19.760
we no longer can be manipulated
by the leftist who wants

00:23:19.760 --> 00:23:22.520
to keep us dumb and motivated by emotion.

00:23:22.520 --> 00:23:25.410
If we understand that faith
is the answer to everything,

00:23:25.410 --> 00:23:26.330
this is a country that was built

00:23:26.330 --> 00:23:28.730
on Judeo-Christian values, period,

00:23:28.730 --> 00:23:30.550
and no matter how we worship him,

00:23:30.550 --> 00:23:32.210
the reason why we look
at each other inside-out

00:23:32.210 --> 00:23:34.520
versus outside-in better
than any other country

00:23:34.520 --> 00:23:35.760
is because we believe
there's a God in heaven

00:23:35.760 --> 00:23:37.840
that makes us want to bless.

00:23:37.840 --> 00:23:40.360
We want the blessings by blessing others.

00:23:40.360 --> 00:23:42.450
Industry; We now understand as a country

00:23:42.450 --> 00:23:43.640
that we are not that country

00:23:43.640 --> 00:23:46.420
that wants a 10 months
vacation, that's just not us.

00:23:46.420 --> 00:23:47.540
We want to work.

00:23:47.540 --> 00:23:48.463
We want to sweat.

00:23:49.851 --> 00:23:51.710
we want to, man, fall flat and get back up

00:23:51.710 --> 00:23:53.150
because we feel good about the process.

00:23:53.150 --> 00:23:54.553
That's who we are and
that's why we've always led

00:23:54.553 --> 00:23:57.624
the entire world in terms
of producing things,

00:23:57.624 --> 00:24:00.130
that's in the DNA of our American people.

00:24:00.130 --> 00:24:02.790
The last but not least is our family.

00:24:02.790 --> 00:24:05.338
The family unit (clears throat) dictates

00:24:05.338 --> 00:24:08.720
where our country goes
and how high we fly,

00:24:08.720 --> 00:24:11.360
and that's why the leftist has
been after us for a long time

00:24:11.360 --> 00:24:13.030
destroying the family unit.

00:24:13.030 --> 00:24:15.030
We should always think about, very simply,

00:24:15.030 --> 00:24:18.460
the way I was brought up,
the best thing I could do

00:24:19.301 --> 00:24:21.470
is I wanna make sure that my family's name

00:24:21.470 --> 00:24:22.490
is one that's respected.

00:24:22.490 --> 00:24:24.793
It's a legacy for my dad, my mom.

00:24:26.399 --> 00:24:27.659
If I do the kind of things,

00:24:27.659 --> 00:24:29.610
where they are proud of
that I carried their name

00:24:29.610 --> 00:24:31.183
and I do the same through to my kids

00:24:31.183 --> 00:24:34.550
then We have done a great
deed for our country

00:24:34.550 --> 00:24:36.660
and for my family and for my race,

00:24:36.660 --> 00:24:39.070
and as a citizen, that all works out.

00:24:39.070 --> 00:24:41.080
So the family is really the key point

00:24:41.080 --> 00:24:42.060
and it would just have

00:24:42.060 --> 00:24:43.400
to get back to understanding what that is

00:24:43.400 --> 00:24:45.710
or what the roles might be
and all that kind of stuff.

00:24:45.710 --> 00:24:47.440
That's a long answer to a short question,

00:24:47.440 --> 00:24:48.818
but I hope I got there, buddy.

00:24:48.818 --> 00:24:49.651
(laughs loudly)

00:24:49.651 --> 00:24:50.700
- I think you got there.

00:24:50.700 --> 00:24:51.533
- Okay.

00:24:51.533 --> 00:24:53.750
- So you mentioned the
meritocracy of the NFL now,

00:24:53.750 --> 00:24:57.210
and I think that goes obviously
for all professional sports

00:24:57.210 --> 00:24:58.043
at this point.

00:24:58.043 --> 00:24:59.810
Nobody cares what color you are,

00:24:59.810 --> 00:25:00.970
it's can you play?

00:25:00.970 --> 00:25:03.330
I mean, and for me, I'm a basketball guy,

00:25:03.330 --> 00:25:05.590
but when I played ball my entire life,

00:25:05.590 --> 00:25:07.310
any court that I showed up to,

00:25:07.310 --> 00:25:10.800
nobody cared if you were
white or black or Asian

00:25:10.800 --> 00:25:11.633
or anything else,

00:25:11.633 --> 00:25:12.466
what do you care about?

00:25:12.466 --> 00:25:14.690
Can the guy put the ball in the hole?

00:25:14.690 --> 00:25:15.894
That's the only thing that you care about.

00:25:15.894 --> 00:25:18.880
And there is a certain--
Just win, baby. (laughs)

00:25:18.880 --> 00:25:20.790
- Yeah, just win, baby, that's it.

00:25:20.790 --> 00:25:24.010
There's a certain beauty
and a simplicity to that,

00:25:24.010 --> 00:25:25.320
but when you see things,

00:25:25.320 --> 00:25:27.790
you know, how politicized
sports has become.

00:25:27.790 --> 00:25:29.790
So you may remember about a year ago,

00:25:29.790 --> 00:25:31.593
there was a big thing in the NBA that

00:25:31.593 --> 00:25:35.320
the players didn't want the
owners to call them owners

00:25:35.320 --> 00:25:38.540
because that somehow had some connotations

00:25:38.540 --> 00:25:40.050
to slavery or something,

00:25:40.050 --> 00:25:43.010
but the simple fact is they own the team.

00:25:43.010 --> 00:25:45.930
I mean, they own the
team, that's how it works.

00:25:45.930 --> 00:25:49.010
When you hear these things
and you hear it from people

00:25:49.010 --> 00:25:51.960
that are multi-millionaires
that make money

00:25:51.960 --> 00:25:53.270
that you could never do.

00:25:53.270 --> 00:25:55.950
Do you remember how much
you made your rookie year?

00:25:55.950 --> 00:25:58.060
- Oh, (laughs loudly) I'm almost ashamed,

00:25:58.060 --> 00:25:58.970
but keep in mind,

00:25:58.970 --> 00:26:01.100
first round draft choice, get ready.

00:26:01.100 --> 00:26:03.270
First round draft choice, the 13th pick,

00:26:03.270 --> 00:26:04.790
the first defensive back.

00:26:04.790 --> 00:26:08.140
My bonus was 50,000,

00:26:08.140 --> 00:26:12.700
and I made 23, 25 or 27,
the first three years,

00:26:12.700 --> 00:26:13.870
and those last three,
- Wow!

00:26:13.870 --> 00:26:15.820
those years were not
guaranteed, by the way,

00:26:15.820 --> 00:26:17.983
had to go out and make that happen,

00:26:17.983 --> 00:26:19.000
- Right.
- But now, it's crazy

00:26:19.000 --> 00:26:21.190
- Now guys you get, like,
an eight-year guaranteed

00:26:21.190 --> 00:26:22.023
100 million.

00:26:22.023 --> 00:26:23.390
- It's crazy.
- But really,

00:26:23.390 --> 00:26:24.470
when you hear these guys say

00:26:24.470 --> 00:26:25.820
these sorts of things,

00:26:25.820 --> 00:26:29.040
and fight about these sorts of things,

00:26:29.040 --> 00:26:32.010
